- matrix4 Test the associative property A(BC) == (AB)C
- matrix4 Test the distributive property A(B+C) == AB + AC
- matrix4 Test the lack of commutative property AB != BA (in general)
- Add all the variations of rotations with a matrix using matrix4_set_from_quaternion, matrix4_set_from_axis_angle, matrix4_transformation_rotation_x,y,z
- add a vector to a quat, add each element one by one
- add a scalar to a quat, add the scalar to each element of the quat
- in quat-slerp is the shortest arc properly computed with negate or conjugate?
- is quaternion_dot_product an appropriate operation on a quaternion?
- add a unit test for quaternion inverse that involves a unit (norm == 1) and a non-unit quaternion (norm != 1)
- make doxygen ignore vector3_get_reference_vector3


Correctness and API safety:
- matrix2/3/4_invert leave the matrix unchanged when it has no inverse (see matrix4_invert);
  consider returning a status the caller can check, and document the behavior
- matrix2/3/4_inverse "has an inverse" check. DECIDED and done: only an exactly zero
  determinant means "no inverse", as in Unity, three.js and Unreal. The old check
  (|det| < HYP_EPSILON) treated valid small-scale matrices as having no inverse (a
  uniform 0.01 scale in single precision).
  Known limitation: rounding can leave a singular matrix with a tiny nonzero
  determinant (~1e-8 float, ~1e-18 double; ~80% of 1000 tested matrices with one row =
  sum of two others), and such matrices are inverted into very large values. The
  inverse functions document this.
  Considered and not chosen: a scale-independent ratio check (|det| / product of row
  lengths, likewise columns, take the larger); no major library uses it.
  Possible later: a separate reliability (condition) estimate function, as in
  LAPACK / MATLAB rcond, so callers who need it can check.
- The same "within HYP_EPSILON of zero" guard is used for vector normalize, quaternion
  normalize / inverse, quaternion_is_pure and the SLERP shortcuts; review each the same way
- Answer the SLERP shortest-arc question above with a test, then fix if needed
- Test quaternion inverse with non-unit quaternions (see above) and fix if needed
- HYP_EPSILON is 1E-5 (single) and 1E-12 (double), compared as an absolute difference.
  Consider relative / ULP-based comparisons for large magnitudes (e.g. the matrix6
  single precision inverse lands at ~2.3e-5)

C89 compliance:
- `gcc -std=c89 -pedantic` reports 32 warnings: 25 unnamed structs/unions, 7 structs with
  no named members. Decided: the anonymous unions stay; the README says "almost 100%
  C89/C90 compliant".
- <stdint.h> / uint8_t are C99

Experimental (_EXP) functions:
- Add reference-value tests (e.g. against GLM output) for projection_perspective_fovy_rh,
  projection_ortho3d_rh, view_lookat_rh, rotate_by_euler_angles, transformation_decompose,
  quaternion_angle_between
- Once tested, promote them and drop the _EXP suffix

API gaps:
- vector2/3/4_lerp
- vector2/3/4 clamp, component-wise min / max
- vector projection (project a onto b)
- matrix4 TRS constructor (translation + rotation + scale in one call)
- normal matrix helper (inverse-transpose of the upper 3x3)
- quaternion <-> matrix3 conversions
- left-handed variants of the _EXP projection / view functions
- a depth-range option for the _EXP projections: 0..1 (Vulkan/D3D) vs -1..1 (OpenGL)
- consider optional by-value inline variants for small types for expression-style code

Tests:
- Randomized / property-based round trips: quaternion vs axis-angle vs matrix rotations,
  invert(invert(M)) ~= M, M * invert(M) ~= I

Remaining casts (README: prefer no casts):
- hypatia.h math macros: DECIDED, keep the 8 casts in HYP_SQRT, HYP_FMOD, HYP_SIN,
  HYP_COS, HYP_TAN, HYP_ASIN, HYP_ACOS, HYP_ATAN2. They are an accepted exception to
  "prefer no casts": they are contained in these macros and callers never see them.
  Why: C89 only has the double functions, so the float build calls sin() and casts the
  result. The alternative (sinf/sqrtf/... per precision) is C99 and, measured with glibc
  on x86-64 over 10M angles: (float)sin is always the closest float (worst 0.50 ulp),
  sinf is worst 0.56 ulp and differs in the last bit 1.4% of the time, and sinf is ~2x
  faster. Trig is rarely the bottleneck here, so accuracy and C89 win. Revisit only if
  float trig speed matters. (Long double will still need sinl etc.; see below.)
- hypatia.h scalar_random_rangef: 2 casts (HYP_RANDOM() and HYP_RANDOM_MAX to
  HYP_FLOAT). Accepted: the single precision build needs them for int -> float, and
  they are contained in that one function.

Random number API:
- Possible later: points inside a disk / ball (GLM diskRand / ballRand; Unity
  insideUnitCircle / insideUnitSphere); a random direction within a cone (Unreal
  VRandCone).

Long double support (planned):
- Add a HYPATIA_LONG_DOUBLE_PRECISION_FLOATS mode: HYP_FLOAT long double, HYP_FLOAT_C(x) x ## L
- Math macros need sinl/cosl/sqrtl/etc. (C99, not C89) in that mode
- The print helpers print every value through hyp_print_value, which takes a double
  and uses %10f; in that mode it needs a long double parameter and %10Lf
- Needs its own HYP_EPSILON
- long double differs by platform: same as double on MSVC, 80-bit on x86 gcc/clang,
  128-bit software on ARM64 Linux; CI needs a long double job per platform

Build and CI:
- Add a strict `-std=c89 -pedantic -Werror` CI job. As it stands it cannot pass, because
  the anonymous unions stay (see C89 compliance).
- The generated docs contain none of the function doc comments: they are in the
  implementation section, which Doxygen skips (HYPATIA_IMPLEMENTATION is not defined
  for it). With HYPATIA_IMPLEMENTATION in PREDEFINED, Doxygen reports 157 "no matching
  file member found" warnings (it pairs `struct vector2 *self` in the declarations with
  `vector2 *self` in the definitions). Options (not decided): move the doc comments to
  the declarations, or find the Doxygen settings that pair them.

Docs:
- Publish the Doxygen output (e.g. GitHub Pages)
- Add a conventions guide: handedness, row- vs column-major storage, angles in radians,
  objects are mutated in place

